note bottom of F
  **Now wired**, on two counts: the processor
  narrows it to ProcessedField, and render
  walks it for reasons. It was amber here
  while neither reader existed.

  Has **no fields left**. Its children were
  ""Vec<Extracted<TransformationExtraction, ..>>"",
  and that type is deleted — it was never a
  node, it held the author's transformation
  expression, which is ""#[from]"".

  Refilled by the derive (#field/children).
end note

note bottom of Y
  Works and is tested, but driven ONLY by
  base/syntax/worked.rs — never by the macro
  path. The module carries an explicit
  ""#![allow(dead_code)]"" saying so.
  (#syntax/not-driven)
end note

note bottom of P
  **Wired.** Concrete impls for both
  extractions, driven from lib.rs.

  Sharpest demonstration of why the source
  rides on the OUTPUT: FieldExtraction's value
  carries NOTHING, so everything ProcessedField
  knows comes from ""input.source()"". Unwrap
  between stages and there is nothing to
  process. (#processor/needs-the-source)
end note

note bottom of G
  **Wired.** An enum fails extraction and the
  impl is STILL emitted — same associated items,
  vacant. Not an empty stream: that would leave
  every use site reporting "no associated item
  named FIELDS" on top of the real error.
  (#generator/stub-is-not-empty)
end note

legend right
  |<#E8F4EA>| live and tested |
  |<#FFF4E5>| works, tested, not wired |
  |<#FFD9D9>| an OPEN QUESTION, not a status |
endlegend

note as Gap
  **The pipeline runs end to end.**
  Every reason in the tree reaches the user.

  Both earlier gaps are closed:

  1. The derives generate extraction logic.
     StructExtraction and FieldExtraction stay
     hand-written where they sit — a proc-macro
     crate cannot use its own derives — so the
     proof lives in the facade instead
     (#derive/cannot-self-host).

  2. ID(syntax/render) descends. ""Diagnose""
     says where a node's children are; the
     ""Extracted"" around each one renders its
     reasons, because it holds the node they
     span against (#render/who-renders).

  The 'sorted by span' clause was DROPPED, not
  done: ""Span::start"" is gated and returns
  line 0 inside a real macro, so a sort would
  be a no-op that looked like a guarantee.
  Depth-first over a source-ordered syn tree
  already IS source order.
  (#render/traversal-is-source-order)

  What remains: the syntax stage is still not
  on the path at all.
end note

Gap .. R

note bottom of R
  A node renders its OWN reasons via its
  ""Extracted""; a VALUE implements ""Diagnose""
  only to say where its children are. So a
  grammar type never learns how a reason
  becomes an error, and the node-span fallback
  cannot be forgotten.

  ""#[derive(Extractor)]"" emits this impl
  alongside — a leaf is the one case it cannot
  state, and omitting it is a compile error,
  never a silent gap.
  (#derive/diagnose-rides-along)
end note

note as Closed
  **#pipeline/relocate-traits is closed.**
  Extractor, Validate and Visitable now live
  in proc_macro_flow_traits, because
  ""#[derive(Extractor)]"" on a user's type means
  they must be nameable from outside.
  ""Sourced"" was deleted rather than moved.
end note

Closed .. Entry

note as Naming #FFD9D9
  **OPEN QUESTION — a naming fork, not a status.**
  This box was called ""lib.rs::extractor"" until the
  derives landed.

  It had to change: two ""#[proc_macro_derive(Extractor)]""
  in one crate is, VERIFIED:
  ""error[E0428]: the name `Extractor` is defined multiple times""
  The new derive took
  the name; this one was renamed after what it emits.

  But the old ""extractor"" was NOT merely a pipeline
  runner. Its own TODO said its expansion was
  ""a stub until ExtractorPipeline::expand exists"" — it was the
  PLACEHOLDER for generating extraction logic, which is
  what ""#[derive(Extractor)]"" now does by another route.

  So the open question is whether those are ONE feature
  or TWO:

  • ONE — the architecture moved from a pipeline struct
    expanded at macro time to declarative attributes on
    the extraction type. Then #extractor/pipeline,
    ""#extractor/expansion"" and ""#extractor/macro-wiring"" 
    should be CLOSED with an answer, not left dangling.

  • TWO — the derive took a reserved name, and the
    derive is what should be renamed instead.

  Four annotations still say ""lib.rs::extractor"" meaning
  the OLD one. They resolve cleanly and mean the wrong
  thing, which is worse than a broken link.
end note

Naming .. DE

